{"t":21125,"dir":"out","frame":{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_probe_1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**The Last Watch**\n\nFor thirty-one years, Elias Moran climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Gannet Point light each evening, wound the clockwork, and watched the lamp bloom across the black water. The ships had changed. Steel hulls replaced the wooden schooners, and radar replaced the lamp's faithful sweep, but Elias kept the brass polished anyway.\n\nThe notice came in October. The light would be automated by spring. A machine in a steel box would do his work, and the Coast Guard thanked him for his service in a letter that used the word \"legacy\" twice.\n\nHe did not argue. He simply kept climbing.\n\nOn his final night, a storm rolled in from the northeast, the kind that tears shingles from roofs and turns the sea white. At nine o'clock, a radio crackled in the lantern room. A fishing boat, the *Marianne*, had lost its engine a mile off the rocks.\n\nElias gripped the radio. \"Hold your heading,\" he said. \"Watch for my light. I'm not going anywhere.\"\n\nHe nursed the generator through the night, hands raw from the cold crank, and called out bearings for three hours, his voice steady as a bell buoy. Below him, the waves threw themselves against the stone like they wanted in.\n\nAt dawn, the *Marianne* limped into harbor, its crew wrapped in blankets on the deck. None of them knew his name. They only knew the light had held.\n\nElias walked down the steps for the last time, counting them as he always had, one hundred and twelve. Halfway down, he stopped and touched the cold iron railing, the way a man touches a horse he has ridden for years.\n\nBehind him, the lamp kept turning, as it always would, for whoever came next and whoever needed it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_token